        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "342145f9-8b35-5c98-8523-369c367c56fa",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Charles White Whittlesey\n- Organizations: United States Army, 326th Infantry Regiment, 82nd Division\n- Events: Meuse-Argonne Offensive, World War I\n- Concepts: Lost Battalion, Medal of Honor\n\nFacts:\n- The Meuse-Argonne Offensive, also called the Maas-Argonne Offensive and the Battle of the Argonne Forest, was a major part of the final Allied offensive of World War I along the entire Western Front.\n- In October 1918 during the final stages of World War I, Charles White Whittlesey led the Lost Battalion in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive.\n- The 326th Infantry Regiment was a United States Army infantry regiment that served during World War I as part of the 82nd Division.\n- The 326th Infantry Regiment fought in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive and was inactivated in 1919.\n- Charles White Whittlesey was a United States Army lieutenant colonel and an American Medal of Honor recipient.\n- Charles White Whittlesey was born on January 20, 1884, and was presumed dead on November 26, 1921.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9d0d902b-daf0-5d41-89f7-d31bd3651df3",
        "text": "Page: Charles White Whittlesey. Sentence: Lieutenant Colonel Charles White Whittlesey (January 20, 1884 – presumed dead November 26, 1921) was a United States Army officer and an American Medal of Honor recipient who led the \"Lost Battalion\" in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive in October 1918 during the final stages of World War I.\nPage: Meuse-Argonne Offensive. Sentence: The Meuse-Argonne Offensive, also known as the Maas-Argonne Offensive and the Battle of the Argonne Forest, was a major part of the final Allied offensive of World War I that stretched along the entire Western Front.\nPage: 326th Infantry Regiment (United States). Sentence: The 326th Infantry Regiment was an infantry regiment of the United States Army that saw active service during World War I, as part of the 82nd Division and fought in the Meuse-Argonne Offensive and was inactivated in 1919.",
